=== 3. Step 2: how long a boot actually takes

Method: the same QEMU line as boot_check, but the serial file is polled every
50 ms and the clock stops when NOVA_BOOT_OK appears (scripts/test-atomic-install.sh
greps the same string). The image is a fresh install of the same release the
sweep installs (tools/atomic_install.py, same manifest, payload and throwaway
key the script leaves in build/c3/), so it is the post-install boot.

Image install, verbatim:
  $ cp build/nova.hdd build/r6/time.hdd
  $ printf '{"release_sequence": 0}' > build/r6/time-state.json
  $ py -3 tools/atomic_install.py install --image build/r6/time.hdd \
      --manifest build/c3/manifest-r1.json --payload-dir build/c3/payload-r1 \
      --trust-key build/c3/test-key.pub --state-file build/r6/time-state.json
  C3-OP 01 stage_kernel_content
  ... (one line per operation, 13 in all)
  C3-OP 13 write_state
  atomic_install: committed release 0.2.0-c3r1 (sequence 1) to slot A: config
  chain 733, kernel /NUCLEUS.A (188682 B, blake2b 2d4c53effefd459f...)
  install_exit=0

Idle, nothing else of mine running:
  $ IMG=build/r6/time.hdd bash build/r6/time_boot.sh
  ovmf-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 8496 ms
  ovmf-2: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 8261 ms
  ovmf-3: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 8351 ms
  seabios-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 3949 ms

Two Python busy loops for 60 s each (2 of 12 cores), same command:
  ovmf-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 9768 ms
  ovmf-2: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 9543 ms
  ovmf-3: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 9829 ms
  seabios-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 4210 ms

Against the 12,000 ms cap:
  OVMF idle        mean 8,369 ms   margin 3,631 ms (30%)
  OVMF +2 hogs     mean 9,713 ms   margin 2,287 ms (19%)
  SeaBIOS idle         3,949 ms    margin 8,051 ms
Three seconds of the OVMF number is Limine's own countdown (its display says
3... 2... 1...). So firmware plus kernel is about 5.4 s idle and about 6.7 s
under two hogs; the countdown is 36% of the measured boot.
This is the arithmetic behind the flake: a 43% slowdown from idle puts OVMF
past the cap, and two background processes already buy 16% of it.

=== 4. The driver used for step 1 (committed here because build/ is not tracked)

  OUT=build/r6; : > "$OUT/summary.txt"
  for i in 01 02 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 10; do
    start=$(date +%s)
    bash scripts/test-atomic-install.sh > "$OUT/run$i.log" 2>&1
    rc=$?
    end=$(date +%s)
    ovmf_split=$(grep -E "BOOT (PASS|FAIL) \(split-root-committed/OVMF\)|SKIP: no UEFI" "$OUT/run$i.log" | head -1)
    ovmf_inst=$(grep -E "BOOT (PASS|FAIL) \(installed-r2/OVMF\)" "$OUT/run$i.log" | head -1)
    fails=$(grep -c "BOOT FAIL" "$OUT/run$i.log")
    { echo "run $i: rc=$rc duration=$((end - start))s boot_fails=$fails"
      echo "  split-root-committed/OVMF: ${ovmf_split:-NO LINE}"
      echo "  installed-r2/OVMF:         ${ovmf_inst:-NO LINE}"; } | tee -a "$OUT/summary.txt"
  done

The timing script (step 2) is build/r6/time_boot.sh; it replicates boot_check's
arguments and polls the serial file, then kills the QEMU it started.

=== 5. A fact the R9 change introduced into this harness

The payload kernel is built with the qemu-exit feature off, so a successful
boot no longer writes port 0x501 and QEMU no longer exits 33: the kernel prints
NOVA_BOOT_OK and halts. `timeout 12` is therefore what ends EVERY boot now,
passing or failing, and rc=124 no longer means "this boot failed"; only the
serial grep does. The numbers show it: 21 boots x 12 s = 252 s, and the ten runs
took 282-295 s each. Before R9 a passing boot ended early, at the kernel's exit.

Consequences worth the lead's attention (scripts/ and R9's check.sh integration
are the lead's, not mine):
- "BOOT FAIL ... rc=124" in a log now looks like a healthy boot's exit code,
  so the serial line is the only discriminator. That is how boot_check already
  judges, so the oracle is still correct, but the log is easy to misread.
- every local full run pays 12 s per boot where it used to pay the boot's real
  length  (about 4-8 s). 21 boots x (12 - 6) s is roughly two minutes per run
  (estimate, from the two boot lengths measured in section 3).

=== 6. Limits, stated rather than papered over

- The machine is shared. I did not verify it was idle during the ten runs, and
  other agents may have been building; the work order's "idle machine" condition
  is therefore not met. The idle timing above was taken with nothing of mine
  running.
- 2 failures in 10 runs is not a rate, it is an existence proof. It is
  consistent with a per-run failure probability anywhere from a few tenths of a
  percent to tens of percent; ten runs cannot narrow it.
- I did not reproduce either failure on demand. Both were caught by the pass/fail
  gate; the serial logs behind them are the script's single shared log and were
  overwritten by the next boot, so only the tails the script prints survive, and
  those are in build/r6/run01.log and run09.log (not tracked) and quoted above.
- The disk under test is the 2026-10-12 factory image (build/nova.hdd) with the
  2026-10-17 kernel payload. I did not rebuild the factory image.
- Not run on Linux/CI. The CI failure since 38998902987 is a different symptom
  (selftest rc=124 on both firmwares) and is reported separately; it is not this
  measurement.

=== 7. Which branch of the 2026-10-16 decision this is

decisions.md, 2026-10-16, "R6: measure first; take the countdown out of test
images, keep the 12 s limit": if the stage 14 images boot a Limine config that
only tests build, set `timeout: 0` there; if they boot the production install
config (the one the C4 bootchain hashes cover), do not change production boot
behaviour for a test, and raise `timeout` instead with a measured reason.

Stage 14 boots the production install config. Evidence, all read-only:
  scripts/build-disk.sh:24   CONF="$KERNEL_DIR/limine.conf"        (production)
  scripts/build-disk.sh:26   CONF="$KERNEL_DIR/limine-selftest.conf"  only when
                             NOVA_TEST=1, which writes build/nova-selftest.hdd
  stage 14 copies build/nova.hdd, not the selftest image
                             (scripts/test-atomic-install.sh:36, 112, 170, 218,
                             278, 293 - every image it boots is that copy)
  kernel/limine.conf:16      timeout: 3        (production config)
  kernel/limine-selftest.conf:6  timeout: 3    (selftest only)
  tools/atomic_install.py, derive_slot_config: "Everything else (timeout,
  serial, module_path, kernel_cmdline) is byte-identical to what already
  boots" - so the installed release inherits the production config's 3 s
  countdown, and the boot after install is a production-config boot.
So the countdown cannot be removed without changing what the owner sees at
boot, which the same decision rules out. The decision's other branch applies:
raise the cap in the harness, next to the measurement.

Numbers that fix the value (section 3):
  OVMF idle 8.4 s, OVMF under two hogs 9.7 s, SeaBIOS 3.9-4.2 s.
  20 s = 2.38x the idle OVMF boot and 2.06x the loaded one. 15 s would be only
  1.79x idle, and the two synthetic hogs already take 16% of the margin.
Stage 5 (the bootchain hashes) is untouched by a harness-only change: no config
byte, no image and no kernel changes.

