=== 10. Follow-up 2026-10-19: does a 12 s miss mean the boot hung?
         (supersedes section 7's proposed value)

Section 7 recommended raising the cap to 20 s from arithmetic alone (idle
8.4 s, +2 hogs 9.7 s). That never tested the premise: a boot that misses
12 s might be SLOW (a bigger cap cures it) or HUNG (a bigger cap only makes
the harness wait longer). This section boots the same images with a longer
window and measures when NOVA_BOOT_OK actually appears. Nothing under
scripts/ changed; the probe and the prototype below live in build/r6/
(gitignored) and are quoted here, as in section 4.

Probe: build/r6/cap_probe.sh - the same QEMU line as boot_check (same image,
same pflash firmware, same -serial file, same grep) but QEMU runs in the
background, the serial file is polled every 50 ms, and QEMU is killed when
the marker appears. The number is the boot's real length; the window is a
ceiling, not a fixed delay.

  $ bash build/r6/cap_probe.sh <label> <window_s> <image> <ovmf|seabios>

Images: build/c3/split.hdd (the split-root-committed state section 2 boots)
and build/r6/time.hdd (an installed release, section 3). Load is synthetic
CPU: N x `bash -c 'while :; do :; done'` for the duration of the batch.

Idle, nothing else of mine running:
  idle-split-ovmf-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 8747 ms (window 25 s)
  idle-split-ovmf-2: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 6585 ms (window 25 s)
  idle-split-seabios-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 3892 ms (window 25 s)
  idle-split-seabios-2: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 3843 ms (window 25 s)
  idle-time-ovmf-1: NOVA_BOOT_OK after 7165 ms (window 25 s)

Half the cores busy (6 hogs on 12 logical CPUs), 12 OVMF boots:
  12649, 12519, 13199, 11228, 11939, 11821, 11849, 11738, 12597, 13220,
  11186, 10990 ms
  mean 12,078 ms, range 10,990-13,220; 5 of 12 above 12,000 ms
  3 SeaBIOS boots: 5571, 5404, 5209 ms

All cores busy (12 hogs), 4 OVMF boots:
  16478, 16905, 21503, 17974 ms
  mean 18,215; 4 of 4 above 12 s; 1 above 20 s
  2 SeaBIOS boots: 6251, 6210 ms

Every one of those boots printed NOVA_BOOT_OK. Five of the twelve at half
load printed it after 12,000 ms: under `timeout 12` each is a "BOOT FAIL"
line, and the boot did nothing wrong.

What this does and does not prove:
- PROVES the failure class exists. On a shared host a healthy boot takes
  11-21.5 s, so the 12 s cap is not a hang detector, it is a load detector.
  The observed 2/210 failures have exactly this shape: Limine ran, and no
  marker had appeared when the cap hit.
- Does NOT prove either observed failure would have passed at 30 s. Neither
  reproduced on demand (section 6), and SeaBIOS stayed <= 6.3 s in every load
  boot above, so run 01's 12 s SeaBIOS miss is not explained by CPU load
  alone; it may be this class or a host-level stall. Ten runs of the changed
  harness are still the only way to say.
- SUPERSEDES section 7's 20 s. One of four fully loaded boots took 21,503 ms,
  above 20 s. 30 s clears the slowest observed boot by 8.5 s (1.4x) and the
  idle mean by 3.8x.

The cost objection, and its answer. Raising the cap to 30 s and leaving
boot_check otherwise alone costs 21 x 30 = 630 s per run, worse than today's
21 x 12 = 252 s. But the cap only has to bound HANGS: a passing boot can be
stopped the instant the marker reaches the serial. Prototype (scratch copy,
NOT applied to scripts/): build/r6/atomic-install-v2.sh is
scripts/test-atomic-install.sh with two changes:
  (1) `timeout 12` -> poll the serial every 50 ms with a 30,000 ms cap and
      kill QEMU at the marker; boot_fail_expected uses the same poll. It still
      waits the full cap for a non-booting image, because "no marker yet"
      must not be read as "refused to boot".
  (2) nothing else; ROOT is adjusted only because the scratch copy sits in
      build/r6/ instead of scripts/.

  $ diff -u scripts/test-atomic-install.sh build/r6/atomic-install-v2.sh
  --- scripts/test-atomic-install.sh	2026-10-09 18:54:33 +0530
  +++ build/r6/atomic-install-v2.sh	2026-10-19 18:48:42 +0530
  @@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
   set -uo pipefail
  
  -ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)"
  +ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)"   # R6 scratch copy lives in build/r6/
   BUILD="$ROOT/build/c3"
   LOG="$BUILD/qemu-serial.log"
   FAILED=0
  @@ -67,18 +67,35 @@
   mkdir -p "$BUILD"
   IMG="$BUILD/c3-ab.hdd"
  
  +CAP_MS=30000
  +poll_for_marker() { # <qemu pid>: run until NOVA_BOOT_OK reaches the serial or
  +                    # the cap expires, then stop QEMU. A PASS therefore costs
  +                    # the boot's real length, not the whole cap.
  +  local pid="$1" waited=0
  +  while [ "$waited" -lt "$CAP_MS" ]; do
  +    grep -q "NOVA_BOOT_OK" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null && break
  +    sleep 0.05
  +    waited=$((waited + 50))
  +  done
  +  kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  +  wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  +  return 0
  +}
  +
   boot_check() { # <name> <image> <extra qemu args...>
     local name="$1" image="$2"; shift 2
     rm -f "$LOG" "$BUILD/qemu-stderr-last.log"
     local img_arg="$(cygpath -w "$image" 2>/dev/null || echo "$image")"
     # NOVA_BOOT_OK lands within seconds of SeaBIOS+Limine; 12 s is ample and
     # keeps the fault-point sweep (one boot per interruption state) affordable.
  -  timeout 12 "$QEMU_BIN" -M q35 -m 512M "$@" \
  +  "$QEMU_BIN" -M q35 -m 512M "$@" \
       -drive "file=$img_arg,format=raw" \
       -serial "file:$(cygpath -w "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo "$LOG")" \
       -display none -no-reboot \
       -device isa-debug-exit,iobase=0x501,iosize=0x02 \
  -    > "$BUILD/qemu-stderr-last.log" 2>&1
  +    > "$BUILD/qemu-stderr-last.log" 2>&1 &
  +  local qpid=$!
  +  poll_for_marker "$qpid"
     local rc=$?
     if grep -q "NOVA_BOOT_OK" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; then
       echo "  BOOT PASS ($name)"
  @@ -94,12 +111,13 @@
     local name="$1" image="$2"
     rm -f "$LOG"
     local img_arg="$(cygpath -w "$image" 2>/dev/null || echo "$image")"
  -  timeout 12 "$QEMU_BIN" -M q35 -m 512M \
  +  "$QEMU_BIN" -M q35 -m 512M \
       -drive "file=$img_arg,format=raw" \
       -serial "file:$(cygpath -w "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo "$LOG")" \
       -display none -no-reboot \
       -device isa-debug-exit,iobase=0x501,iosize=0x02 \
  -    > /dev/null 2>&1
  +    > /dev/null 2>&1 &
  +  poll_for_marker "$!"
     if grep -q "NOVA_BOOT_OK" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; then
       echo "  SABOTAGE GATE FAIL ($name): mutant image BOOTED - the suite is decorative"
       return 1

Run (no synthetic load; other agents may have been active, as in section 6):
  $ start=$(date +%s); bash build/r6/atomic-install-v2.sh > build/r6/v2-run1.log 2>&1; rc=$?; end=$(date +%s); echo "v2 rc=$rc duration=$((end-start))s"
  v2 rc=0 duration=183s
  $ grep -c "BOOT PASS" build/r6/v2-run1.log
  21
  $ grep -E "BOOT FAIL|SABOTAGE|oracle:|fault-point sweep" build/r6/v2-run1.log
  fault-point sweep: 15 states checked, 0 failed
    SABOTAGE GATE PASS (mutant/SeaBIOS): mutant image refused to boot
  c3-atomic-install oracle: PASS

21 of 21 boots pass, the negative gate still refuses the mutant, and the run
took 183 s against 283-295 s for the ten runs in section 2 (35-38% less). The
sweep now spends a SeaBIOS boot's real 4-8 s instead of 12 s at the cap, so
the larger hang window costs no wall-clock time; it buys the margin.

Recommendation for scripts/test-atomic-install.sh (lead-owned):
  take the two hunks above - the 50 ms marker poll, cap 30,000 ms, in both
  boot_check and boot_fail_expected. A failing boot then costs the full 30 s;
  that is the price of not calling a slow boot a failure. Re-running section
  2's ten runs against the changed harness is what closes R6.

Limits:
- The load is synthetic (busy loops). It reproduces CPU starvation, not disk
  contention, AV scanning or WHPX scheduling, which may matter more for QEMU.
- One v2 run under light load is a smoke test, not evidence about the ~1%
  flake. The changed harness still has to be run ten times.
- The numbers are this machine (12 logical CPUs) on this date. Section 2's
  idle OVMF boots were 8.5/8.3/8.4 s; today's were 8.7/6.6/7.2/7.5 s, so the
  machine has not drifted much.
- 5 of 12 above the cap at half load is not a flake rate: the hogs are a
  chosen load, not the load other agents happened to put on the box on
  2026-10-17.

