  $ grep -h "BOOT PASS" build/r6/sweep2/run*.log | wc -l
  210

Against section 2, same 21 boots per run, same images and same kernel payload:

  section 2 (timeout 12):   283-295 s per run, 2,863 s total, 208/210 boots
  section 11 (poll, cap 30): 172-178 s per run, 1,751 s total, 210/210 boots

The 38% saving is the marker poll doing its job: a SeaBIOS boot that used to
sit out the whole 12 s cap now ends in its real 4-6 s.

What 0 of 210 does and does not prove:
- It is consistent with section 10's mechanism being the whole story, and the
  negative gate still works: the mutant image never prints the marker, so it
  still waits the full 30 s and is still refused in all ten runs. The change
  does not simply pass everything.
- It does NOT prove the flake rate is zero. At the 2/210 rate section 2
  observed (0.95%), a clean 210-boot run happens with probability
  0.9905^210 = 0.13. Two clean samples are the difference between "the 12 s
  cap demonstrably mislabels healthy boots" (proven in section 10) and "the
  flake is gone" (not proven).
- It is a scratch copy run from build/r6/, not the committed script, so the
  repository's behaviour is unchanged until the lead applies the hunks.

State of R6 after this: the diagnosis is measured (section 10), the proposed
change is measured (this section), and the remaining act belongs to the lead
- apply section 10's two hunks to scripts/test-atomic-install.sh, then run the
sweep once against the committed file for the record. No repo file was
touched by this follow-up; build/r6/sweep2/ holds run01.log .. run10.log and
summary.txt.
